[human]: How to use CSS to change the color of jokes to red?
[bot]: To change the color of the jokes to red using CSS, you can add a style for the paragraph (`<p>`) element that displays the joke. You can do this by including a `<style>` tag within the `<head>` section of your HTML document. Here's how you can modify the provided HTML code to achieve this:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Random Joke Generator</title>
    <style>
        /* CSS to change the joke text color to red */
        #jokeDisplay {
            color: red;
        }
    </style>
    <script>
        // Function to show a random joke
        function showRandomJoke() {
            // Array of jokes
            var jokes = [
                "Why don't scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything.",
                "I told my wife she should embrace her mistakes. She gave me a hug.",
                "Why do we tell actors to 'break a leg?' Because every play has a cast.",
                "Parallel lines have so much in common. It’s a shame they’ll never meet."
            ];

            // Generate a random index based on the number of jokes
            var randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length);

            // Display the joke
            document.getElementById("jokeDisplay").innerHTML = jokes[randomIndex];
        }
    </script>
</head>
<body>

<h2>Random Joke Generator</h2>
<button onclick="showRandomJoke()">Tell me a joke</button>
<p id="jokeDisplay"></p> <!-- Joke will be displayed here -->

</body>
</html>
```

In this modification, the `<style>` tag contains CSS that targets the element with the ID `jokeDisplay`, which is the paragraph element where the joke is displayed. The `color: red;` property changes the text color of the jokes to red. This CSS will be applied as soon as the page loads, so any text that appears in the `#jokeDisplay` element, including the jokes, will be red.

